<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<section xml:id="one-way-anova">
  <title>Inference for More than Two Means</title>
  
  <subsection>
    <title>Comparing Several Population Means</title>

    <definition>
      <title>One-Way Analysis of Variance</title>
      <statement>
        <p><term>One-way analysis of variance</term>, or <term>one-way ANOVA</term>, compares population means for groups defined by one categorical explanatory variable. The response variable is quantitative. The categorical variable is the <term>factor</term>, and its categories are the <term>levels</term>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>Let <m>k</m> be the number of groups, <m>n_i</m> the sample size in Group <m>i</m>, and <m>N=n_1+\cdots+n_k</m> the total sample size. The group population means are <m>\mu_1,\ldots,\mu_k</m>.</p>  

    <assemblage>
      <title>Hypotheses for One-Way ANOVA</title>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>H_0:\mu_1=\mu_2=\cdots=\mu_k</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>H_a:\text{At least one population mean differs.}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>The alternative does not say that every mean differs from every other mean. Rejecting the null establishes evidence of some difference; follow-up comparisons identify which differences are supported.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <assemblage>
      <title>Requirements for Classical One-Way ANOVA</title>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>One quantitative response is measured. Groups are formed by one factor, and data come from an appropriate random sample.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The populations within groups are approximately normal.</p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>The population variances are approximately equal across groups.</p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </assemblage>  
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Variation and the ANOVA Table</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Between-Group and Within-Group Variation</title>
      <statement>
        <p><term>Between-group variation</term> describes how far the group sample means are from the overall mean. <term>Within-group variation</term> describes how far individual observations are from their own group mean. ANOVA compares these sources of variation.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The <term>grand mean</term> is the mean of all observations, <m>\bar x_{\mathrm{grand}}=\dfrac{\sum_{i=1}^k n_i\bar x_i}{N}</m>. A <term>sum of squares</term> (SS) measures variation. A <term>mean square</term> (MS) is a sum of squares divided by its degrees of freedom.</p>

    <assemblage xml:id="av-calculations">
      <title>Relationships Used to Complete an ANOVA Table</title>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>SST=SSG+SSE</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>dfG=k-1,\qquad dfE=N-k,\qquad dfT=N-1</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>MSG=\dfrac{SSG}{dfG},\qquad MSE=\dfrac{SSE}{dfE}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        <md number="no">
          <mrow>F=\dfrac{MSG}{MSE}</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Under the null hypothesis and the model requirements, compare the statistic with <m>F_{k-1,N-k}</m>. The p-value is the right-tail area.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <table>
      <title>One-Way ANOVA Table</title>
      <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Source</cell>
          <cell>df</cell>
          <cell>SS</cell>
          <cell>MS</cell>
          <cell>F</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Between groups</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>k-1</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SSG</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SSG/dfG</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>MSG/MSE</m>
          </cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>
            <line>Within groups</line>
            <line>(error)</line>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>N-k</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SSE</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SSE/dfE</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>—</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell>Total</cell>
          <cell>
            <m>N-1</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>
            <m>SSG+SSE</m>
          </cell>
          <cell>—</cell>
          <cell>—</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
    <p>A larger F-statistic means the group means are more separated relative to the variation within groups. R labels the within-group row <c>Residuals</c> and usually omits the Total row. Empty F and MS entries in the Total row are not missing calculations.</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>One-Way ANOVA in R</title>

    <p>Put the response in one numeric columnand the group labels in a factor column. The formula <c>response ~ group</c> tells R which variable is the response and which variable defines the groups.</p>

    <program language="r">
data = c(data1,data2,data3,...)

group = factor(c(rep("Group1", length(data1)), rep("Group2", length(data2)), 
rep("Group3", length(data3)),...))

summary(aov(data~group))
</program>

    <p>Read the factor row in <c>summary()</c>. Its <c>F value</c> is the test statistic and its <c>Pr(&gt;F)</c> entry is the p-value. </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="av-examples">
    <title>ANOVA Examples</title>
    <example xml:id="av-summary-example">
      <title>Growth Media: A Partially Completed Table</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Independent cultures are randomly assigned to one of three growth media, with 10 cultures per medium. The response is biomass after a fixed time, in grams. A researcher claims that all three population mean biomasses are equal. Assume the ANOVA requirements are reasonable. The following summaries and partial ANOVA table are supplied; use the displayed relationships to fill the blanks.</p>
        
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Medium</cell>
            <cell>n</cell>
            <cell>Mean biomass</cell>
            <cell>Sample SD</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>A</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>12.0</cell>
            <cell>2.0</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>B</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>14.0</cell>
            <cell>2.0</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>C</cell>
            <cell>10</cell>
            <cell>16.0</cell>
            <cell>2.0</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
        
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
          <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>Source</cell>
            <cell>df</cell>
            <cell>SS</cell>
            <cell>MS</cell>
            <cell>F</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Medium</cell>
            <cell>___</cell>
            <cell>80</cell>
            <cell>___</cell>
            <cell>___</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Residuals</cell>
            <cell>27</cell>
            <cell>108</cell>
            <cell>___</cell>
            <cell>—</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Total</cell>
            <cell>___</cell>
            <cell>___</cell>
            <cell>—</cell>
            <cell>—</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>

        <p>
          Fill in the rest of the table and test the researchers claim at <m>\alpha = 0.10</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="av-raw-example">
      <title>Seedling Growth</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Twenty-four seedlings are randomly assigned to Control, Nutrient A, or Nutrient B, with eight seedlings per group. Each seedling is grown independently under otherwise comparable conditions. The response is final height in centimeters. A biologist claims that at least one population mean differs. Test the claim at <m>\alpha=0.05</m></p>
        
        <tabular halign="center" row-headers="yes">
          <row>
            <cell>Control</cell>
            <cell>18</cell><cell>22</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>25</cell>
            <cell>19</cell><cell>23</cell><cell>21</cell><cell>20</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>NutrientA</cell>
            <cell>24</cell><cell>28</cell><cell>26</cell><cell>31</cell>
            <cell>25</cell><cell>29</cell><cell>27</cell><cell>26</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>NutrientB</cell>
            <cell>25</cell><cell>29</cell><cell>27</cell><cell>32</cell>
            <cell>26</cell><cell>30</cell><cell>28</cell><cell>27</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="av-output-example">
      <title>Enzyme Activity: Interpreting Output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Twenty-four independent cultures are randomly assigned to three preparation methods, eight per method. The response is enzyme activity in units per liter. A researcher claims that at least one population mean differs. A condensed ANOVA output table is shown below.</p>
        <table xml:id="av-enzyme-output">
          <title>ANOVA Output for Enzyme Activity</title>
          <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" valign="middle" top="medium" bottom="medium">
            <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
              <cell>Source</cell>
              <cell>Df</cell>
              <cell>Sum Sq</cell>
              <cell>Mean Sq</cell>
              <cell>F value</cell>
              <cell>Pr(&gt;F)</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Method</cell>
              <cell>2</cell>
              <cell>4.75</cell>
              <cell>2.375</cell>
              <cell>0.5816</cell>
              <cell>0.5677</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Residuals</cell>
              <cell>21</cell>
              <cell>85.75</cell>
              <cell>4.083</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
              <cell>—</cell>
            </row>
          </tabular>
        </table>
        <p>
          <ol marker="a)">
            <li>
              <p>Identify the numerator and denominator degrees of freedom and the test statistic.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>At <m>\alpha=0.05</m>, do the data support the researcher’s claim?</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>Does your decision establish that all three population means are equal? Explain.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
    <title>Bonferoni Comparisons</title>

    <definition>
      <title>Bonferoni Pairwise Comparisons</title>
      <statement>
        <p><term>Bonferoni comparisons</term> compare each pair of group population means while accounting for the collection of comparisons.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <program language="r">
pairwise.t.test(data,group,p.adjust.method = "bonferroni")
</program>

    <example xml:id="av-tukey-raw">
      <title>Seedlings: Generate and Interpret Tukey Output</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Using the ANOVA model you fitted to the raw seedling heights, run Bonferonni's follow-up test to compare all three pairs of treatment groups. Which pairs show a statistically significant difference in mean seedling height?</p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
